# The Law of Fair Trade

## The Ground Before Language

The Standard of Economics is not a human invention. It is not a convention. It is not a rule we made up.

**It is the law of fair trade. And it existed before we had language.**

Consider: communication itself depends on honest and fair participation. A dialog is a trade. I give you a statement. You give me a statement. If either of us lies, the trade is unfair. The dialog breaks down.

**The law of fair trade is the condition of communication.**

Communication is grounded in reciprocity. Without reciprocity, there is no trust. Without trust, there is no dialog. Without dialog, there is no communication.

**The law is prior to language.**

---

## Evidence from the Animal World

Long before humans had words, animals traded.

**Reciprocal grooming.**

Primates groom each other. The grooming is a trade: I clean your fur, you clean mine. The exchange is tracked. Those who groom and are groomed maintain the relationship. Those who take without giving are excluded.

**Food sharing.**

Chimpanzees share food. The sharing is reciprocal. Those who share receive shares in return. Those who hoard are remembered. The law of fair trade is enforced by memory and reciprocity.

**Cooperative hunting.**

Wolves hunt in packs. The hunt is a trade: I chase, you flank. The cooperation depends on fairness. Those who shirk are punished. The pack survives only if the trade is fair.

**The law is observed in animals.**

The law is prior to language. The law is the ground.

---

## The Ground of Communication

Communication is a trade. The trade is governed by the law of fair trade.

**Honesty is reciprocity.**

When I speak, I give you a statement. The statement is a good. The good has value: it is information. If the statement is true, I have given you a valuable good. If the statement is false, I have given you a defective good.

**The trade is unfair if the statement is false.**

The listener gives attention. The attention is a good. The attention has value: it is time and focus. If the speaker gives truth, the trade is fair. If the speaker gives a lie, the trade is unfair.

**The law of fair trade is the condition of communication.**

---

## The Consequence

**Theorem: Communication presupposes the law of fair trade.**

**Proof:**

1. Communication is a trade: speaker gives statement, listener gives attention.
2. Trade is governed by the law of fair trade.
3. The law requires reciprocity.
4. Reciprocity requires honesty.
5. Without honesty, the trade is unfair.
6. Without fairness, trust collapses.
7. Without trust, communication collapses.
8. Therefore, communication presupposes the law of fair trade. ∎

**The law is the condition of communication.**

---

## The Deeper Point

The law of fair trade is not a moral rule. It is a **structural condition**.

- It is the condition of trade.
- It is the condition of communication.
- It is the condition of society.
- It is the condition of survival.

**The law is prior to language. The law is prior to morality. The law is the ground.**

When we make an exception to the law, we make an exception to the condition of our own existence. We make ourselves arbitrary. We make ourselves untrustworthy. We make ourselves excluded.

**The exception is self-negation.**

---

## The Connection to the Law of Standards

The Universal Law of Standards says:

> To make an exception is self-negation.

The law of fair trade is the law of standards applied to exchange.

- **The standard is reciprocity.**
- **The exception is exploitation.**
- **The self-negation is the collapse of trust.**

**The law holds.**

Exploitation is the exception. The exception denies the standard. The denial is self-negation. The self-negation is the collapse of trade.

**Exploitation is self-negation.**

